{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "proof", "parent_tag": "0032", "placeholders": {"FOOTNOTECLOSE_0001": "}", "FOOTNOTEOPEN_0001": "\\footnote{", "MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{I}_0$", "MATH_0002": "$M_0 : \\mathcal{I}_0 \\to \\mathcal{I}$", "MATH_0003": "$M : \\mathcal{I} \\to \\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0004": "$M \\circ M_0 : \\mathcal{I}_0 \\to \\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0005": "$\\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0006": "$\\mathcal{C}^{opp}$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{categories-definition-system-over-poset}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{categories-lemma-cofinal}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{proof}\n", "suffix": "\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:9140012929781c30fb93f789b5eef15f6054e65fad425deff700d5b40c721cbd", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:ff79a78cc65655e4d7d926047b38e3a76c89d031b1e2327a8841d800016ac542", "source_text": "As explained in the text following Definition <REF_0001>, we may view preordered sets as categories and systems as functors. Throughout the proof, we will freely shift between these two points of view. We prove the first statement by constructing a category <MATH_0001>, corresponding to a directed set<FOOTNOTEOPEN_0001>In fact, our construction will produce a directed partially ordered set.<FOOTNOTECLOSE_0001>, and a cofinal functor <MATH_0002>. Then, by Lemma <REF_0002>, the colimit of a diagram <MATH_0003> coincides with the colimit of the diagram <MATH_0004>, from which the statement follows. The second statement is dual to the first and may be proved by interpreting a limit in <MATH_0005> as a colimit in <MATH_0006>. We omit the details.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:0399cf9d03c63cf03120d1ff5455a87316b375bb8050db996b740e1d2a71ad5e", "unit_id": "tag:0032:proof-p001"}
{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "proof_paragraph", "parent_tag": "0032", "placeholders": {"EMCLOSE_0001": "}", "EMOPEN_0001": "{\\em ", "MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0002": "$F$", "MATH_0003": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0004": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0005": "$F$", "MATH_0006": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0007": "$\\mathcal{I}$", "MATH_0008": "$\\mathcal{I}$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\medskip\\noindent\n", "suffix": "\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:10190291461f5b2431fb0002868ebf520ce83a126be48d57048a8928b7d4e3e2", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:47d1c9938f0f56e0ca2673f046704c6f9f206572005f3efe3e71f5b32eb123bb", "source_text": "A category <MATH_0001> is called <EMOPEN_0001>finitely generated<EMCLOSE_0001> if there exists a finite set <MATH_0002> of arrows in <MATH_0003>, such that each arrow in <MATH_0004> may be obtained by composing arrows from <MATH_0005>. In particular, this implies that <MATH_0006> has finitely many objects. We start the proof by reducing to the case when <MATH_0007> has the property that every finitely generated subcategory of <MATH_0008> may be extended to a finitely generated subcategory with a unique final object.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:a0cb262b03499e866e2b10a18c187b25505d732b789f5cf573e268aa19d865f4", "unit_id": "tag:0032:proof-p002"}
{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "proof_paragraph", "parent_tag": "0032", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\omega$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathcal{I}\\times \\omega$", "MATH_0003": "$\\Pi : \\mathcal{I} \\times \\omega \\to \\mathcal{I}$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\medskip\\noindent\n", "suffix": "\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:454cd92864ffee4e4945fc983d7dcc9021b41e307b091ad68e2183dd174a14f8", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:b8183482ad652f9416b62f0cce2fde53b3b2f0bab2bb1e6e928015b5e1e0b40b", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> denote the directed set of finite ordinals, which we view as a filtered category. It is easy to verify that the product category <MATH_0002> is also filtered, and the projection <MATH_0003> is cofinal.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:e5a75f75d98224cc2f8309ac01862d33a5aa39b12e41ff5acdafa0abb3cbf676", "unit_id": "tag:0032:proof-p003"}
{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "proof_paragraph", "parent_tag": "0032", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathcal{I}\\times \\omega$", "MATH_0003": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0004": "$(\\{f_i\\}, i_\\infty)$", "MATH_0005": "$\\mathcal{I} \\times \\omega$", "MATH_0006": "$\\mathcal{F} \\to \\mathcal{I} \\times \\omega$", "MATH_0007": "$f_i : i \\to i_\\infty$", "MATH_0008": "$i$", "MATH_0009": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0010": "$f : i \\to i'$", "MATH_0011": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0012": "$f_i = f_{i'} \\circ f$", "MATH_0013": "$i_\\infty$", "MATH_0014": "$i_\\infty$", "MATH_0015": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0016": "$f_i$", "MATH_0017": "$\\mathcal{I}$", "MATH_0018": "$\\omega$", "MATH_0019": "$\\mathcal{F}^+$", "MATH_0020": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0021": "$i_\\infty$", "MATH_0022": "$\\text{id}_{i_\\infty}$", "MATH_0023": "$f_i$", "MATH_0024": "$i_\\infty$", "MATH_0025": "$\\mathcal{F}^+$", "MATH_0026": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0027": "$\\mathcal{F}^+$", "MATH_0028": "$\\mathcal{F}^+$", "MATH_0029": "$\\mathcal{I}$", "MATH_0030": "$i_\\infty$", "MATH_0031": "$M : \\mathcal{I} \\to \\mathcal{C}$", "MATH_0032": "$M\\circ\\Pi$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{categories-lemma-cofinal}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\medskip\\noindent\n", "suffix": "\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:09d551812a372a804c0d318639833c93adce516e8b332e03907ce884ada294ce", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:1f53c767dbe59ad357ceb5474ef032a95d5d81f5139997291a02034190908f88", "source_text": "Now let <MATH_0001> be any finitely generated subcategory of <MATH_0002>. By using the axioms of a filtered category and a simple induction argument on a finite set of generators of <MATH_0003>, we may construct a cocone <MATH_0004> in <MATH_0005> for the diagram <MATH_0006>. That is, a morphism <MATH_0007> for every object <MATH_0008> in <MATH_0009> such that for each arrow <MATH_0010> in <MATH_0011> we have <MATH_0012>. We can also choose <MATH_0013> such that there are no arrows from <MATH_0014> to an object in <MATH_0015>. This is possible since we may always post-compose the arrows <MATH_0016> with an arrow which is the identity on the <MATH_0017>-component and strictly increasing on the <MATH_0018>-component. Now let <MATH_0019> denote the category consisting of all objects and arrows in <MATH_0020> together with the object <MATH_0021>, the identity arrow <MATH_0022> and the arrows <MATH_0023>. Since there are no arrows from <MATH_0024> in <MATH_0025> to any object of <MATH_0026>, the arrow set in <MATH_0027> is closed under composition, so <MATH_0028> is indeed a category. By construction, it is a finitely generated subcategory of <MATH_0029> which has <MATH_0030> as unique final object. Since, by Lemma <REF_0001>, the colimit of any diagram <MATH_0031> coincides with the colimit of <MATH_0032> , this gives the desired reduction.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:114e97ab13fe62fe181eed9a2d4e009a00908b2fc07c08915fc29611024443b7", "unit_id": "tag:0032:proof-p004"}
{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "proof_paragraph", "parent_tag": "0032", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{I}$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathcal{I}_0$", "MATH_0003": "$M_0 : \\mathcal{I}_0 \\to \\mathcal{I}$", "MATH_0004": "$\\mathcal{F} \\subset \\mathcal{F'}$", "MATH_0005": "$\\mathcal{I}_0$", "MATH_0006": "$\\mathcal{F}$", "MATH_0007": "$\\mathcal{F}'$", "MATH_0008": "$\\mathcal{I}$", "MATH_0009": "$\\mathcal{I}$", "MATH_0010": "$\\mathcal{I}$", "MATH_0011": "$\\mathcal{I}_0$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\medskip\\noindent\n", "suffix": "\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:c8d7ccbef9f6946fadbc95e1b3e9b7260cbfdd78b87c38de4b9a9b3232f80b4c", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:ee8c4547730fd4fc19bf11473134fb6537e26aecb36e3a6e792fd15c907a6276", "source_text": "The set of all finitely generated subcategories of <MATH_0001> with a unique final object is naturally ordered by inclusion. We take <MATH_0002> to be the category corresponding to this set. We also have a functor <MATH_0003>, which takes an arrow <MATH_0004> in <MATH_0005> to the unique map from the final object of <MATH_0006> to the final object of <MATH_0007>. Given any two finitely generated subcategories of <MATH_0008>, the category generated by these two categories is also finitely generated. By our assumption on <MATH_0009>, it is also contained in a finitely generated subcategory of <MATH_0010> with a unique final object. This shows that <MATH_0011> is directed.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:4d2b5238388d78047b8aa700025ec8081d8afa09ed7cdfa0ab7600df79f17106", "unit_id": "tag:0032:proof-p005"}
{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "proof_paragraph", "parent_tag": "0032", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$M_0$", "MATH_0002": "$\\mathcal{I}$", "MATH_0003": "$M_0$", "MATH_0004": "$i$", "MATH_0005": "$\\mathcal{I}$", "MATH_0006": "$\\mathcal{F}_1, \\mathcal{F}_2$", "MATH_0007": "$\\mathcal{I}_0$", "MATH_0008": "$\\varphi_1 : i \\to M_0(\\mathcal{F}_1)$", "MATH_0009": "$\\varphi_2 : i \\to M_0(\\mathcal{F}_2)$", "MATH_0010": "$\\mathcal{I}$", "MATH_0011": "$\\mathcal{F}_{12}$", "MATH_0012": "$\\mathcal{F}_1$", "MATH_0013": "$\\mathcal{F}_2$", "MATH_0014": "$\\varphi_1, \\varphi_2$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{categories-definition-cofinal}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\medskip\\noindent\n", "suffix": "\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:1196a311cb6551bfb0469dcbd54d28e75255b7229298333e02b7f31a251fb947", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:bc36fd72ad212bcd1014411950b14018919c0033891e9969e556a08783cdda83", "source_text": "Finally, we verify that <MATH_0001> is cofinal. Since any object of <MATH_0002> is the final object in the subcategory consisting of only that object and its identity arrow, the functor <MATH_0003> is surjective on objects. In particular, Condition (1) of Definition <REF_0001> is satisfied. Given an object <MATH_0004> of <MATH_0005>, objects <MATH_0006> in <MATH_0007> and maps <MATH_0008> and <MATH_0009> in <MATH_0010>, we can take <MATH_0011> to be a finitely generated category with a unique final object containing <MATH_0012>, <MATH_0013> and the morphisms <MATH_0014>. The resulting diagram commutes", "source_text_hash": "sha256:8182f2fe02e8ac50546a71f6711bc0da1d4d6e20546bf493f45280bb955a5ba0", "unit_id": "tag:0032:proof-p006"}
{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "display_math", "parent_tag": "0032", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$$\n\\xymatrix{\n& M_0(\\mathcal{F}_{12}) & \\\\\nM_0(\\mathcal{F}_{1}) \\ar[ru] & & M_0(\\mathcal{F}_{2}) \\ar[lu] \\\\\n& i \\ar[lu] \\ar[ru]\n}\n$$"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": "\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:7285cd45479ceb06c9dd03b7202ebcc931abfc01c7c181f925d7073e0fc83234", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:751dba63313f4517b1f3573a4afb6e6bde503330c6078e9fdffe49eeac4cfaae", "source_text": "<MATH_0001>", "source_text_hash": "sha256:f624e62d0a08cb4d4f7609fd8d992c88c0c527d3d910b8338b5f898e6261de67", "unit_id": "tag:0032:proof-display001"}
{"chapter": "categories", "node_kind": "proof_paragraph", "parent_tag": "0032", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{F}_{12}$", "MATH_0002": "$M_0(\\mathcal{F}_{12})$"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": "\n\\end{proof}\n\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:55a559f6817a38088f75de3f06ef6998b574cafd0a6f8c62a8bb98aef3bf5ff8", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:ae66602ff0ea0f6d95cc45633522c60beceed5b459bad23f75c3868ac0c61a12", "source_text": "since it lives in the category <MATH_0001> and <MATH_0002> is final in this category. Hence also Condition (2) is satisfied, which concludes the proof.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:fce931e4384d8749e907afda7eb45e5a7fe35d9f33ff8a14fea6489a072ab134", "unit_id": "tag:0032:proof-p007"}
